Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Bay Seven Leaves Two Snares Empty
- Story & Context: At 168 BPM, Nalin opens with a four-note chakhe phrase built from tonic, fifth, upper second, and a short downward fret slide. Ellis removes two expected snare ghosts after the final attack, creating the first Break Vacancy. Ploy answers the upper note with one prepared-Rhodes suspended-second dyad, then stops. Arun replaces a missing hat with one dry woodblock hit. Lena enters only after the second phrase, taking the lowest note into a chalumeau fundamental. The first major lift is a Dry Return where the complete edited break comes back immediately after the chakhe decay ends, without fill or riser.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Loading bay marked 7, two blank snare boxes on a freight rhythm chart, dark wooden chakhe beside an old brass cargo scale, river reflections beyond the open door. Sparse fragments: “bay seven,” “leave two empty,” “hold the fret,” “return dry.”

Track 2: Three Bronze Steps Above the Tide Mark
- Story & Context: At 164 BPM, the title track develops Register Handoff. Nalin plays a repeated low-middle-high chakhe cell; Ploy continues the high note as a clipped Rhodes fifth, Lena catches the middle register with a short bass-clarinet falling second, and muted electric bass answers the low note once per four-bar cycle. The drums become denser only between handoffs. Midway through, a wide fret slide is given nearly half a bar of Decay Clearance, making the jungle groove feel as if it briefly bends around the zither rather than stopping. The section resolves with three consecutive Dry Returns, each shorter than the previous one.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Three bronze warehouse steps descending toward a painted tide mark, three instrument-register lines passing from wood strings to keyboard keys to clarinet tubing, red customs stamp half-submerged in river water. Sparse fragments: “three steps,” “above the tide,” “answer below,” “pass it on.”

Track 3: Ferry Ledger Closes Before the Break Returns
- Story & Context: The closer runs at 160 BPM and begins with the sparsest drums on the EP. Nalin plays isolated chakhe dyads separated by long decays while Ellis uses only kick, one dry snare, and occasional ghost fragments. Arun adds soft klong thap palm strokes inside the empty spaces. Ploy remains silent for the first minute. Lena uses breath-only attacks under the longest phrase endings. In the final section, Ellis gradually increases break density, but every expected full return is interrupted by one last acoustic phrase. The final chakhe note slides downward and rings into the room; the break never comes back. The EP ends with string decay, dock ambience, and one distant woodblock reflection.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Old ferry ledger closing over a page of incomplete breakbeat notation, final drum row left blank, one bronze plectrum resting on dark wood beside a river-depth scale. Sparse fragments: “ledger closed,” “break withheld,” “last ferry,” “do not return.”

